  <p>
    While consumer web maps and smartphones use spherical angular coordinates (<a href="/tools/latitude-longitude/">Latitude and Longitude</a>), civil engineers, land surveyors, topographers, and military tacticians require metric Cartesian grids.
  </p>
  <p>
    The most ubiquitous metric system in global GIS is the <strong>Universal Transverse Mercator (UTM)</strong> projection. In this guide, we demystify how UTM divides the curved Earth into flat zones, how false eastings eliminate negative numbers, and how geodetic datums like <strong>WGS 84, NAD83, and ETRS89</strong> ensure millimeter precision.
  </p>

