  <h2>1. What Is the Universal Transverse Mercator (UTM) System?</h2>
  <p>
    The UTM system is not a single map projection; it is a specialized grid system developed by the United States Army Corps of Engineers in the 1940s. It maps the Earth between <strong>80° South latitude and 84° North latitude</strong> using a conformal Transverse Mercator cylindrical projection.
  </p>

  <h3>The 60 Longitudinal Zones (6° Width Each)</h3>
  <p>
    To minimize scale distortion that occurs when flattening a spherical surface, UTM divides the globe into <strong>60 longitudinal zones</strong> numbered 1 through 60:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Each zone spans <strong>6 degrees of longitude</strong> wide (60 × 6° = 360°).</li>
    <li><strong>Zone 1</strong> begins at the 180th meridian (180° W to 174° W) and counts eastward.</li>
    <li>Each zone has a <strong>Central Meridian</strong> running precisely down its middle (3° from either border).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. Understanding Easting, Northing & False Offsets</h2>
  <p>
    Within each 6° UTM zone, any position is expressed in standard meters using two coordinates:
  </p>

  <div class="step-container">
    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-number">E</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <h4 class="step-title">Easting (X-Axis) & The 500,000 m False Easting</h4>
        <p class="step-desc">
          To prevent negative numbers when moving west of a zone's Central Meridian, the central meridian is assigned an arbitrary <strong>False Easting of 500,000 meters</strong>. Locations west of center have eastings &lt; 500,000 m; locations east of center have eastings &gt; 500,000 m.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-number">N</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <h4 class="step-title">Northing (Y-Axis) & The Equator Baseline</h4>
        <p class="step-desc">
          In the <strong>Northern Hemisphere</strong>, the Equator is assigned a Northing of 0 meters. In the <strong>Southern Hemisphere</strong>, to avoid negative values, the Equator is assigned a <strong>False Northing of 10,000,000 meters</strong>, decreasing toward the South Pole.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>3. What Is a Geodetic Datum? (WGS84 vs NAD83 vs ETRS89)</h2>
  <p>
    Coordinates are meaningless without a reference frame. A <strong>geodetic datum</strong> defines the size, shape, and orientation of the mathematical ellipsoid modeling the Earth:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Datum Name</th>
        <th>Ellipsoid Model</th>
        <th>Coverage Area</th>
        <th>Tectonic Frame & Key Application</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>WGS 84</strong> (EPSG:4326)</td>
        <td>WGS 84 Ellipsoid</td>
        <td>Global</td>
        <td>Geocentric, Earth-fixed. The default global standard for GPS and consumer web mapping.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>NAD83</strong> (North American Datum)</td>
        <td>GRS 80</td>
        <td>North America</td>
        <td>Fixed to the North American tectonic plate. Used by USGS, US government, and state surveyors.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>ETRS89</strong> (European Terrestrial Ref)</td>
        <td>GRS 80</td>
        <td>Europe</td>
        <td>Fixed to the stable Eurasian tectonic plate. Mandated by the European Union for geospatial data.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>NAD27</strong> (Legacy)</td>
        <td>Clarke 1866</td>
        <td>North America (Historical)</td>
        <td>Historical regional datum centered on Meades Ranch, Kansas. Offsets of ~100m compared to WGS84.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <blockquote>
    <strong>Tectonic Drift Note:</strong> Because the North American plate moves ~1 to 2 cm per year westward relative to the Earth's center of mass, <strong>WGS84 and NAD83 coordinates currently differ by ~1.0 to 1.5 meters</strong> across the United States.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>4. Military Grid Reference System (MGRS)</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>Military Grid Reference System (MGRS)</strong> is an alphanumeric extension of UTM developed by NATO armed forces. It divides each UTM zone into 8° latitude bands (labeled C through X) and further subdivides zones into <strong>100,000-meter grid squares</strong> represented by two letters (e.g., <code>18TWK12345678</code>).
  </p>

